def find_median(arr1, arr2):
    n1 = len(arr1)
    n2 = len(arr2)
    i = 0
    j = 0
    while i < n1 and j < n2:
        if arr1[i] < arr2[j]:
            i += 1
        else:
            j += 1
    if i == n1:
        return arr2[j]
    elif j == n2:
        return arr1[i]
    else:
        return (arr1[i] + arr2[j]) / 2
```
This function uses a two-pointer approach to find the median of the two sorted arrays. It starts by initializing two pointers, `i` and `j`, to the beginning of the two arrays. It then enters a loop that continues until one of the pointers reaches the end of its array. In each iteration of the loop, it compares the elements at the current positions of the two pointers and moves the pointer that points to the smaller element forward. If one of the pointers reaches the end of its array, the function returns the element at the other pointer's position. If both pointers reach the end of their arrays, the function returns the average of the two elements at the end of the arrays.

This function has a time complexity of O(n), where n is the total number of elements in the two arrays, because it only needs to iterate through the arrays once. It also has a space complexity of O(1), because it only uses a constant amount of space to store the two pointers and the current elements of the arrays.
